<launch>
  <!-- GPD grasp detection for the Husky + UR5 + Robotiq 2F-140 sim.
       Fed by master_control.py, which publishes a gpd_ros/CloudSamples message
       (cropped cloud in ur5_base_link + camera view_point + target samples) on
       /gpd_cloud and reads grasps back on /clustered_grasps. -->
  <node name="detect_grasps" pkg="gpd_ros" type="detect_grasps" output="screen">

    <!-- 0: PointCloud2, 1: CloudIndexed, 2: CloudSamples -->
    <!-- 0 = plain sensor_msgs/PointCloud2, NOT 2 (CloudSamples).
       CloudSamples looks like the right message , it carries the "points at which to search"
       that we want , but gpd_ros's CloudSamples path is BROKEN and takes the node down with it.
       Its initCloudCamera() sizes an Eigen matrix as Zero(view_points, cloud->size()) and then
       writes camera_source(msg.camera_source[i], i) for every i in msg.camera_source; gpd's
       Cloud constructor takes a PointCloudRGB (field "rgb", float) while gpd_ros hands it a
       PointCloudRGBA (field "rgba", uint32). The result is an out-of-bounds write with no bounds
       check, heap corruption, and a SIGSEGV a few frames later inside Cloud::setSamples. Verified
       under gdb, with a message proven well-formed (17019 points, 17019 camera_source,
       1 view_point, 204 samples, all sizes matching).
       The plain-cloud path uses a different constructor, Cloud(cloud, 0, view_points), with no
       camera_source at all, and does not crash. We get "search only the detonator" by simply
       SENDING only the detonator: see grasp_mtc/scripts/gpd_grasp_server.py. -->
    <param name="cloud_type" value="0" />
    <param name="cloud_topic" value="/gpd_cloud" />

    <!-- optional separate samples topic (unused: samples ride in CloudSamples) -->
    <param name="samples_topic" value="" />

    <!-- Workspace-local config (built-in Eigen LeNet classifier) -->
    <param name="config_file" value="$(find gpd_ros)/config/husky_ur5_gpd.cfg" />

    <param name="rviz_topic" value="plot_grasps" />

  </node>
</launch>
